WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.735 --> 00:00:01.800
- [Announcer] Documentary
for the Cecil Hotel.

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.300
- [Man] Is there something
evil going on here?

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.030
- There's a neighborhood in Los Angeles

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.170
that we need to talk about,

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:09.573
it's called Skid Row.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.000
Last month, there was this really popular

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.910
true crime documentary on Netflix.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.110
It was like the number
one most watched show

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.370
here in the United States
and maybe around the world.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:22.840
And it was about this hotel

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.500
that was right here in Skid Row,

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.190
it's about a 21 year old Canadian woman

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.240
who disappeared after exhibiting

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.130
some very strange behavior in an elevator.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.680
And yes, there's gonna be
plenty of spoilers here.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.240
- [Woman] The Hotel Cecil is content

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.630
and it's got some sort of uncanny force.

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:40.030
- The show is haunting, and confusing,

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:45.030
and thrilling, and an
appalling and insensitive abuse

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.570
of the power of documentary filmmaking.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.030
The whole series feels like
a true crime documentary

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.280
but there ends up being no crime at all.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.970
And the whole energy of this series

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.810
is founded on the idea that this hotel

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.763
is haunted with dark energy.

00:00:58.763 --> 00:01:01.660
- [Man] I think that
we're hoping for a ghost

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.503
because it's what we've come to expect.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.970
- I'm here to tell you
that the Cecil Hotel

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.300
in Los Angeles doesn't have dark energy,

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:16.160
that's a tantalizing, but
very lazy explanation.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.100
And it exonerates the people and policies

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.270
that built modern day LA.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.070
Instead, there's a much bigger story here.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:25.530
One that the documentary decides

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.800
to largely ignore or mischaracterize.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.510
The story worth telling
here about this hotel

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.340
and the neighborhood in which it resides

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:37.090
is the story of how
cities draw boundaries.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:39.210
These subtle borders that are meant to

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:42.670
contain the city's least wanted people.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.520
Once you understand what this looks like

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.680
and how it happened in LA,

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.100
you can understand how it
happens in a lot of cities

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.320
and it helps you understand

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.056
what's really going on
with the Cecil Hotel.

00:01:53.056 --> 00:01:55.350
(upbeat music)

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:56.630
- [Narrator] It's the story of a city

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:58.777
in the microcosm of the "American Dream."

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.510
- Okay, first, let's establish

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.640
what and where the Cecil Hotel is in LA.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:07.970
Here's Downtown LA,

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:09.640
and you have this Financial District here

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.010
with all these big buildings.

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:14.670
A few streets over it,
you have the Cecil Hotel,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.220
right on the border of this

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.160
50 city block neighborhood
called Skid Row,

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.400
right in the heart of Downtown LA.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.250
Go to Skid Row today and you are quickly

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.840
brought into the plight of basic survival

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.530
for nearly 5,000 homeless people.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.260
- The reason why I come to
so many tents out of here

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:33.200
is because people can't
afford to live in a house.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.130
- Nearly half of them
are considered to have

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.300
serious mental illness

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.600
and life expectancy here is 48 years old,

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.210
30 years less than the US average.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.500
This small block of streets is surrounded

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.050
by highly developed districts

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:50.213
that every year become more
shiny and more expensive.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:53.110
So how did Skid Row,

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.340
this one small block of Downtown

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:57.680
become where so many of the city's sick

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.257
and marginalized gathered?

00:02:59.257 --> 00:03:01.840
(upbeat music)

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:05.290
Way back when California

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:07.150
was first taken from Mexico

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:08.720
to become part of the United States,

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.160
loads of Americans were moving West,

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:12.450
moving to California.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.260
Some of them were looking for gold

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.450
and some were just
looking for a new start.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:18.420
The US used to be really
good at railroads,

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:21.230
and they built a railroad
across the entire country.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.780
That railroad ended right here in LA,

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.610
right in the location
of modern day Skid Row.

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.640
- [Reporter] There is
probably no single area

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.810
holding so much of charm and beauty

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:33.937
and the good things of life
as Southern California.

00:03:33.937 --> 00:03:35.990
- [Johnny] We see a lot of people arriving

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:38.900
around this train
station, looking for work.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:40.810
This area developed a reputation as being

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.090
full of transient people
coming to the West

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:46.080
with dreams of gold and a new start,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.820
and instead ending up with
neither, no jobs, no homes.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.457
It was a part of town
that no one wanted to go.

00:03:53.457 --> 00:03:54.470
- [Reporter] Los Angeles County

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:56.203
has a gregarious fun zone

00:03:56.203 --> 00:03:58.540
where everybody has a good time.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:02.620
- As LA grew, so did the
bad reputation of Skid Row

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.740
and so did the homeless population.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.080
The neighborhood was sort
of for the untouchables

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.250
and when anyone considered helping them

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:12.630
or figuring out a policy to
solve this, people resisted.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.270
One columnist of the day wrote, quote,

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.020
don't help this class,

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:20.460
it is a crime against
the community to do so.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:24.320
The Cecil Hotel was eventually
constructed in the 1920s

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:25.800
right here on the outskirts

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.820
of what was becoming this
undesirable part of town.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:31.800
At the time, it was a Grand Hotel

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.930
built for business travelers.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:38.000
What turned the Cecil
Hotel from this to this

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.670
wasn't dark energy or mysterious forces,

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:42.830
nor was it the fact that

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:44.900
YouTube played a concert on the roof

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.348
right next to the Cecil
Hotel in the late '80s.

00:04:47.348 --> 00:04:50.190
(rock music playing)

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:51.200
No, no, no, no, no, no.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:52.950
What happened to the Cecil Hotel

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:53.870
is something that happened

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:57.360
to this whole neighborhood,
starting in the 1970s.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.600
When city planners and politicians

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.460
started reinvisioning Downtown LA,

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:05.290
drawing lines on maps to contain

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:09.550
the growing population of
mentally ill and homeless.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:11.020
The goal here was to bulldoze

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:13.250
all the decaying buildings in Downtown

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:15.470
and to make way for a
new business district,

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:17.580
full of tall shiny buildings.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.810
The original demolition
plan included Skid Row,

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.120
but the community leaders fought back

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:24.810
saying, listen city of LA,

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.270
if you demolish Skid Row

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.570
without solving the underlying issues,

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.890
you're just gonna have a
bunch of smaller Skid Rows

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:33.160
pop up scattered throughout the city.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:34.710
Homelessness isn't gonna go away

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.700
if you just bulldoze the neighborhood,

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:39.250
the city was like, yeah, you have a point.

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:42.480
So they agreed upon setting firm borders,

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:44.920
that would be the quote, containment zone

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:47.030
where the homeless and mentally ill

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:49.340
could stay so that the
surrounding neighborhoods

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.350
could build nice big office buildings

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:54.730
without the presence of these
undesirable populations.

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:58.320
Within Skid Row, they
constructed public bathrooms

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.790
and benches and public spaces

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:03.530
so that as the city officials said,

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:05.180
those amenities could quote,

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:07.330
act as a magnet to hold

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:11.163
the undesirable population
elements in Skid Row.

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:15.940
Undesirable population elements, wow,

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:17.670
that is just a fantastic way

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:21.391
to take human beings and
turn them into units.

00:06:21.391 --> 00:06:23.570
(bells chiming)

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:24.530
So weird.

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:26.630
Even though the borders of Skid Row

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:28.720
based on the city plan
were not like walls,

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:30.350
they were just city streets

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:31.830
but the city planners made sure

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:33.039
that they would have quote,

00:06:33.039 --> 00:06:35.900
'strong edges' that will act as a buffer

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:39.290
between Skid Row and the
rest of the central city.

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:42.080
They promised that when Skid Row residents

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.940
enter the buffer, the
psychological comfort

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:48.870
of the familiar Skid Row
environment will be lost.

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:50.480
So how did they do this?

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:53.230
One way they did this
is with harsh lighting.

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:54.063
Back in the old days,

00:06:54.063 --> 00:06:55.840
they had these historic
lights on main street

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:57.510
which is one of the borders of Skid Row.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.550
They replaced those with
like harsh prison lights

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:04.290
to make it very, just unpleasant
to walk across the street.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.800
They put locks on the trash cans

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.120
in the surrounding districts

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:09.720
so people wouldn't leave Skid Row

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.830
to come scavenge for food.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:14.240
Residents of Skid Row also reported that

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.460
police officers would be
patrolling these borders

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:17.810
and anytime someone would cross

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:19.270
they would be approached,

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:21.220
yet, they would be left alone for a time

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:24.080
if they stayed peaceful
within the containment zone.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:25.380
All of this happened throughout

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.530
the '70s, '80s and '90s

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.720
as rent prices started to go up

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:31.160
all throughout this area.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:33.140
So rent is getting more expensive,

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:35.840
at the same time, funding for
mental health institutions

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:39.150
was drying up and the war on drugs

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:42.180
propagated by the Reagan
Administration was also heating up.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.100
Skid Row literally
became a dumping grounds

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:46.780
for mental health institutions

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:49.260
who would drop off patients right here

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:51.610
knowing that they would be contained.

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:53.870
The idea of saving Skid Row

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:55.660
by drawing borders around it

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:59.140
came from the community
leaders of Skid Row itself.

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:01.810
It was a solution to
keep affordable housing,

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:03.170
keep the community intact,

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:06.180
but soon this solution
was used as an excuse

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.700
to neglect the people within these lines.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:10.700
Street cleaners and trash collectors

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:13.230
came less frequently
than in the rest of LA.

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:15.230
City maintenance for streets

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:16.890
sort of stopped showing up,

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:18.250
but the one city resource

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:21.190
that did show up in
abundance was the police,

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:23.660
whose job it was to keep these quote,

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:27.570
undesirable population
elements within this zone,

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:30.630
criminalizing the poverty
inside these lines,

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:33.150
and yes, here's the Cecil Hotel

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:36.830
right within these firm
containment borders.

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:39.870
So if you look at the Cecil
Hotel under this lens,

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:44.420
you now see the danger,
the drugs, the violence,

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.920
not being caused by
some creepy dark energy

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.150
that lured in this woman from Canada.

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:53.350
Instead, this was the result of a policy

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:56.210
intentionally creating
borders within the city

00:08:56.210 --> 00:09:00.150
to keep the city's problems
contained into one area.

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:02.260
A spot that had real culture

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.650
and community values and history,

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:07.530
and yet because of these
dehumanizing policies,

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:09.130
Skid Row became defined

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:12.570
as a series of statistics
and social problems.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:14.700
What I've found by looking
into these policies

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.210
is that this wasn't an
inevitable course of action,

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:19.180
the city could've put its money

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:21.670
and effort behind the harder thing to do,

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:23.630
which is addressing the underlying issues

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:27.410
of unemployment, mental
illness, drug addiction,

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:29.970
and unaffordable housing stock.

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.000
And yet the policy they pursued

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.040
was fixing this problem with a band-aid,

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:38.680
using policing and the
power of redevelopment.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:40.750
The real problems were swept under the rug

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:42.680
to be dealt with another day,

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:45.250
keeping the city's problems contained

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:48.862
neatly in this border and
hiding them from view.

